Is that skill or opportunism? Truly skillful players usually get that way 
because they don't just grab the first appealing tactic that happens to fall in 
their laps. They meticulously experiment and learn. Eventually they develop a 
winning strategy based on a wide variety of features and contingencies. 
Poly-piling and its near-irresistibility rips a huge hole in this delicate 
process. 

>If a player is too stubborn to use a feature of the game,
>that's his problem.

Wanting a game to be challenging is a problem???

>Just think what a really skilled player could do if he
>used the "giveaway" features.... he would probably blow away all of the
>yahoos who actually won a game.

So you at least admit that these "features" make a *huge* difference in score.
(And you also differentiate between "skilled players" and "those who use the 
giveaway features". Just a slip, perhaps?)

>I'm sure glad I'm not "honest".  I would 
>probably be frustrated and spend all of my time posting requests to eliminate
>polymorph, wands of wishing, etc.

You'd rather spend all of your time posting requests to keep it??? Or is your
point that people should blindly accept the devteam's notion of "the perfect 
game" instead of discussing possible changes?

>Also, there was a suggestion for a 'W' (win) command.  For the "honest" players
>you'll need a 'L' (lose) command.  Get a prompt (really lose?) and get backk
